A||N||12||Ditz88GT||This one has me baffled!||||5||
Z||000000||Ditz88GT||02-28-2000||10:19 AM||||Last night my temp gauge goes through the roof.  Overheat city!  So I pull over and let it cool down.  When it was cool I limped it to the kwiki mart and replaced the lost coolant.  I decided to pull the thermostat, I was really just planning on getting it home.  The temp stayed at 100!  So I figured that the stat was bad.  I put a new one in and the temp went right up to 250+ again.  I then pulled the stat and the temp went back to 100.  First I thought it was the water pump, but the temp stays so low with no thermostat.  Then I thought it was a bad stat, but the stat was open when I pulled it.  Any ideas??  I'm stumped!||||reg||5
Z||000001||88 Database Nut||02-28-2000||02:59 PM||||Well, we have the same situation.  Here's my story.  <P>Car ran cool forever.  One day on way home, overheated like u said.<P>Replaced thermostat, refilled coolant, overheated again.  However, I am sure it was an air pocket.<P>Removed thermo,added water, car ran cool for several days, then O'heated again.  Hmmmmmm.  Refilled coolant, drove for a week, no problem.  Put themostat back in. drove for a week, no problem.  Then it overheated again.  This time I got to the front and felt the radiator, which was cool in center and hot on  edges.  Water pump is two years old, has only a very slight amount of play in shaft, no leaks.  I suspect the radiator is somewhat clogged up and will remove and have it rodded out.  Could also be a head gasket leak, but not likely since no white smoke in exhaust, and no water in oil, and nice steady idle.<BR>With newish water pump and ALL hoses, it's just GOT to be the radiator.  But, we'll see.<P>Good Luck to You (and Me).||||reg||5
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||02-28-2000||04:15 PM||||Air pocket. Check all your hoses and conections. Even though they don't leak water, it doesn't mean you don't have a leak. Water with enough pressure will become a mist and evaporate causeing an air pocket within the system but will show no signs of leakage. When was the last time your hoses were replaced? You should replace them every few years. If this isn't the problem you at least get the peace of mind that your hoses wont burst! Flush your system and follow the factory filling instructions. To explain why it runs cool with no thermostat is simple, you have less resistance to over come in the system so the air pocket actualy moves about instead of sticking around the water pump area. This is my best advice without seeing the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||02-28-2000||04:19 PM||||Also check your water pump for weeping, (dripping from the bottom under the shaft).<BR>One other thought, have you hit anything on the bottom? check for kinked tubes under the car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ditz88GT||02-28-2000||04:29 PM||||Thanks for the help, but I have one more question.  If it is an air pocket, which makes sence, how do I get rid of it?  Will drain and refill work?||||reg||5
Z||000005||88 Database Nut||02-28-2000||04:48 PM||||I agree with you all on Ditz's problem, but if he does that and still has a problem then you've described my situation!  All new hoses, new caps front and back, new water pump (with hoses two years ago), no leaks, no coolant usage (coolant level was right where it was supposed to be when checked).  One thing I was thinking about was that maybe the impeller was working loose and would occaisionally stop working when hot (it's a metal imp too).  Other than that, with no kinked tubes, and a week or more of care free driving, all I can think of now is the clogged radiator.  <BR>Ditz, if you do replace the hoses, don't forget the two heater hoses in the back, which are pressed onto the pipes.  One is right in front of the battery, the other is directly below it but easy to get to from the bottom.  I use a dremel to cut them loose (takes about 30 seconds of slow cautious cutting).  Also, the heater hoses up front probably need to come out. Unfortunately, none of these hoses is available in preformed versions (back ones can be replaced with regular heater hose, front ones have multiple bends but can still use regular hose).  Two sizes, 5/8ths and 3/4 inches.  All main hoses available from carparts.com for a very reasonable price (right now they are 33% off everything!).  ||||reg||7
Z||000006||daved||02-28-2000||07:06 PM||||I would suggest you check the rad cap as well.  If the vacuum side check valve on the cap is leaking, the liquid will more readily boil, as the pressure escapes.  Had weird cooling difficulties for some time till I discovered that pesky little thing!  Cheap fix though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick's Dad||02-28-2000||07:33 PM||||I had something similar to this, but I had the "benefit" of visible leakage. It was the crossover pipe, a rolled steel tube that runs from under the driver's butt diagonally between the engine and the firewall up behind the passenger's head. This, though is for the V-6's only, and according to Associated Auto's Fiero Secrets I, all the other pipes are stainless. <P>Oh, yeah. To get it out, you need to drop the A/C compressor and both of its brackets.||||reg||1
Z||000008||sdm1964||02-28-2000||08:20 PM||||Hey i feel your pain, I just got the radiator rotted out new pump, belt, thermo and it was fine till i sat at an idle today, It went to 220and then made a funny noise in the catch can, I started back up and let cool for for 3 or 4 minuites.That dexi-coolant cost me 7.00 a gallon. the only thing i did not replace was the rad cap, i went and got one for 4.00 I hope that is it. There is no way it was air, I almost had the nose on the ground when i filled it from the back, started, cooled down and rechecked. We will find out tommorrow.||||reg||8
Z||000009||88 Database Nut||02-29-2000||08:06 AM||||Thanks guys.  The thing about this problem that baffles me so much is that I have never ever had this happen before, and it disappears for a week or more at a time!  I thought maybe the caps were weeping under pressure, so had replaced them at the start of all this mess.  I have new hoses and water pump from two years ago (when I had the engine out to do a clutch job) and also know that the cross over pipe is like new.  With no coolant leaking, either internally or externally, I have come to the conclusion that it's either a clogged rad (which is probable from the above mentioned hand test), or the impeller on the 2 year old water pump is slipping occaisionally. My approach to maintenance on the car has been to replace things or repair them in advance due to the age factor, so I have no problem pulling the rad and having it cleaned out, since it's given 13 years of service and no doubt needs attention.  However, I have a sneaking suspicion that the water pump is the real problem....||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||02-29-2000||08:37 AM||||88 db n & Ditz,<P>When you did all of that system filling, was the rear of the car elevated?  Your problem really sounds like an air pocket to me.  Depending on where the air is in the system it will cause different hot spots.  Often they are very localized.  If they are not in the general vicinity of your temp. sensor then your gage will not necessarily be affected.  Also when they move near the temp sensor they can cause very quick and irradic heating.  <P>I would seriously reccomend draining some coolant then jacking the back up as high as you can and fill as high as possible.  Then start the car and let idle for a minute or two.  Then double check the level of coolant at the back.  Continue this process untill additional coolant cannot be added.  At that point you should have successfully "burped" your coolant system.  <P>Otherwise you could be causing cavitation somewhere by a kinked hose/tube although it is highly unlikely.  If the inner diameter is redced greatly it is possible to actually cause localized boiling (happens even with room temperature water) since the boiling point is both a function of temperature and pressure.  More likely though if a kinked hose or tube is present is simply a huge flow restriction.  I do not think that this is the case since it is not a constant problem.  This case scenerio would be one of a constant problem (at least in theory).<P>William T. Thorn, III<P>||||reg||5
Z||000011||87PegasusGT||02-29-2000||03:49 PM||||I had same problem. Done all of the above. Dealership told me head gasket though I see no smoke or milky oil. I think that is right though, because of water? on my tip insides.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ditz88GT||03-01-2000||01:19 PM||||Well, let me start by saying thanks to everyone who helped me out here.  I got bad news today...HEADGASKET!  This is bad, looks like the gasket is letting air/fuel into the cooling system and causing air pockets.  I reason it's sometimes cool and sometimes not is because the air pockets work there way around the system to the water pump.  Once the air is at the pump, it keeps the pump from pumping water around and then the temp goes up.  Thanks again everyone.||||reg||1
